{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to take every change to agreed requirement text, including a rewording for precision and the removal of a requirement, through the same review and agreement before committing it.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** The `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to send every change to agreed requirement text through the same review and agreement before committing it. The text names two cases explicitly: a rewording for precision and the removal of a requirement.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reviews_every_change_to_agreed_text]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It makes three assertions:\n- The skill's front-matter description, read through `description`, contains \"before changing code, tests, or requirement text\". This shows the skill is triggered before requirement text changes.\n- `step_of` finds \"Sort the new and changed requirements into groups\" in workflow step 3. So step 3 is the review step. `step_of` and `workflow_steps` parse the numbered workflow steps in `SKILL.md`.\n- `assert_phrases` finds this exact sentence in the bundled skill: \"Every change to agreed text, including a rewording for precision or removing a requirement, goes through steps 3 to 5 before it is committed\". `bundled_skill` collapses whitespace, so the sentence matches although it wraps across lines in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`. The sentence covers both named cases, rewording and removal.\n\n**Does \"steps 3 to 5\" mean review and agreement?** In `SKILL.md`, step 3 is Review, step 4 is Present with the Gate (agreement), and step 5 is Commit. The test pins step 3 as review. Other tests in the same file cover the rest:\n- `the_requirements_skill_commits_agreed_requirements_before_code` checks the order draft < review < gate < commit < implement.\n- `the_requirements_skill_gates_commit_and_code_on_every_group_being_decided` checks the Gate text.\n\nThose tests carry other `{test}` references. They give indirect support that steps 4 and 5 are agreement and commit, but this test does not pin those step numbers. I judge that a minor point, not a gap. The obligation is that the skill directs this, and the assertion on the directive sentence enforces it. Dropping the sentence, dropping the rewording or removal cases, or pointing it at the wrong range (such as \"3 to 4\") would make the test fail.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. There are no acknowledgments, and none are needed.",
